---
/**
 * /about
 *
 * SEO carried over verbatim: title, description, canonical, BreadcrumbList.
 *
 * ONE THING IS DELIBERATELY NOT A STRAIGHT PORT. The legacy timeline signals
 * its unshipped item ("Q4 2026 — Regulatory monitor") with `opacity-50` and
 * nothing else, and marks the current one with a teal border and a pulsing dot.
 * Both states are carried by appearance alone. That fails WCAG 1.4.1, and it
 * has a content consequence that matters more than the guideline: read by
 * anyone who cannot see the dimming — a screen reader, a high-contrast user, or
 * anyone skimming — a planned feature reads exactly like a delivered one. On a
 * page whose entire job is credibility, and on a site that has already had to
 * strip overclaims, that is the wrong failure to inherit.
 *
 * Each entry therefore carries an explicit textual status. "Planned" is not new
 * information; it is the information the opacity was already trying to convey.
 *
 * ── COPY PASS, 2026-08-02 (owner decision). DESIGN UNCHANGED ────────────────
 *
 * 1. "Engineers" is gone. The owner's own description is consulting and bidding
 *    experience from 2010 on multi-million pound bids. The obvious replacement,
 *    "consultants", fights the page's own Vision, which promises a supplier can
 *    answer a tender "without paying a bid-writing consultancy": naming
 *    ourselves as the thing the reader is avoiding is an own goal. "Bid
 *    practitioners" claims the doing without claiming that profession.
 *
 * 2. The 2010 experience is stated as a dated FACT IN THE BODY, never in the
 *    heading, and every occurrence says in the same breath that it is the
 *    founders' prior record and not something this software produced. There are
 *    no customers, so none are named or implied, and no win rate is quoted
 *    anywhere. Refusing that claim is the position, so Values now says so.
 *
 * 3. Market-neutral narrative, UK public as the PROOF POINT rather than the
 *    identity. The eyebrow led with jurisdiction, the standfirst scoped the
 *    product to "the UK procurement rules", and Mission and Vision were both
 *    UK-only. The mechanism now leads and the UK specificity is demoted, not
 *    deleted, matching MarketShape.astro on the homepage.
 *
 * 4. Accuracy. PPN 002 is published 13 February 2025 and mandatory from
 *    1 October 2025 (24 February 2025 is the Procurement Act commencement date,
 *    a different fact), the minimum weighting is 10%, and s.52 and s.71 are
 *    stated as two separate duties everywhere they appear. The Q4 2026 entry no
 *    longer opens with the word "Live", which read as delivered next to a chip
 *    saying it is not.
 *
 * SEO IS DELIBERATELY UNTOUCHED. title, description and canonical are the
 * legacy values verbatim and tests/parity.spec.js hard-fails on any drift from
 * about.html, which this task may not edit. The description still says
 * "London-based team"; changing it is a separate, measured SEO decision.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/layout/Section.astro';
import Button from '../components/ui/Button.astro';
import NewsletterForm from '../components/forms/NewsletterForm.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'About Us | CrowAgent';
const description =
  'A London-based team that reads the UK procurement rules so CrowMark applies them the same way every time. Company details, history and what we stand for.';

const CALENDLY = 'https://calendly.com/crowagent-platform/30min';
const COMPANIES_HOUSE =
  'https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/17076461';

const schema = [
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'About', path: '/about' },
  ]),
];

/**
 * Two cards, as before. The old pair was "The approach" and "What you get", and
 * they said the same thing twice: both opened on starting from the actual
 * regulation rather than a template. That freed one card for the substantiation
 * the new heading needs, and the duplication is merged into the other.
 */
const APPROACH = [
  {
    eyebrow: 'Who builds it',
    body: 'The founders have been writing and winning bids since 2010, including multi-million pound contracts taken from tender through to delivery. That is their own record before CrowAgent existed, not a result this software produced. What that work teaches is narrower and more useful than any headline: most marks are lost where the evidence already exists and nobody can point to it.',
  },
  {
    eyebrow: 'How it is built',
    body: 'Every feature starts from the source rule rather than a template. PPN 002 for social value, and the two separate KPI duties in the Procurement Act 2023: s.52 sets and publishes at least 3 KPIs on contracts over £5m, and s.71 assesses performance against them. Each answer carries the clause it was written against, and every figure is checked against your own confirmed data before it is shown, or the draft is rejected.',
  },
];

/**
 * Mission and Vision were both scoped to the UK, which contradicts the
 * market-neutral narrative. UK public is kept as the proof point, in Mission
 * only, so the same sentence is not made twice.
 *
 * Values now carries the refusal explicitly. The register to define against
 * advertises a "241% increase in success rates"; not making that class of claim
 * is a position, and a position has to be stated to count.
 */
const PRINCIPLES = [
  {
    kicker: 'Mission',
    heading: 'Make bidding answerable, whatever your size',
    body: 'Any supplier should be able to answer a requirement from evidence it already holds, whatever its size or budget, with figures the software cannot invent and a named person on every approval. UK public procurement is where we prove it, because its duties are published.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'Vision',
    heading: 'Answers you can defend, first time',
    body: 'A market where any supplier can answer a tender requirement from evidence they already hold, cited to source, without paying a bid-writing consultancy to assemble it.',
  },
  {
    kicker: 'Values',
    heading: 'Statute over speculation',
    body: 'Every figure cites its source. A figure that cannot be traced to your own confirmed data is rejected rather than shown, nothing is rounded into place to fill a gap, and we do not quote win rates, ours or yours.',
  },
];

/** `status` is rendered as text, never as opacity alone. See the note above. */
type Status = 'done' | 'now' | 'planned';
const TIMELINE: {
  when: string;
  heading: string;
  body: string;
  status: Status;
  link?: { label: string; href: string };
}[] = [
  {
    // 2010, not 2021. This is the dated form of the fact the heading no longer
    // claims, and the second sentence is load-bearing: it is the founders'
    // record, not CrowAgent's, and it names nobody because there is nobody to
    // name.
    when: '2010 to 2025',
    heading: 'Founding team experience',
    body: 'Bid and tender work inside regulated industries from 2010, including multi-million pound bids written, won and delivered to completion. That is the founders’ own record before CrowAgent, not a result of the product. It is also where we saw at first hand how much of the friction smaller suppliers face is evidence that exists and cannot be found.',
    status: 'done',
  },
  {
    when: 'Jan to Feb 2026',
    heading: 'Regulatory research',
    body: 'Deep dive into the statutory frameworks: PPN 002, published 13 February 2025 and mandatory from 1 October 2025, and the KPI duties in the Procurement Act 2023 at s.52 and s.71. Every figure has to trace back to a named, citeable source.',
    status: 'done',
  },
  {
    when: 'March 2026',
    heading: 'CrowAgent founded',
    body: 'Incorporated as CrowAgent Ltd in England and Wales (Company No. 17076461). Engine prototyping begins.',
    status: 'done',
    link: { label: 'Company 17076461 on Companies House', href: COMPANIES_HOUSE },
  },
  {
    when: 'May 2026',
    heading: 'Platform live',
    body: 'CrowMark in live production.',
    status: 'now',
  },
  {
    when: 'Q4 2026',
    heading: 'Regulatory monitor',
    // The word "Live" opened this sentence while the chip beside it said the
    // item is not built. Two signals, one of them wrong. The tense now agrees
    // with the status.
    body: 'A regulatory change feed for UK public procurement, surfacing material updates straight into your dashboard once it ships.',
    status: 'planned',
  },
];

const STATUS_LABEL: Record<Status, string> = {
  done: 'Delivered',
  now: 'Live now',
  planned: 'Planned, not yet built',
};

const COMPANY = [
  { label: 'Registered name', value: 'CrowAgent Ltd' },
  { label: 'Registered in', value: 'England and Wales' },
  { label: 'Company number', value: '17076461', mono: true },
  { label: 'Data protection', value: 'ICO registered data controller' },
  { label: 'Data residency', value: 'UK and EU' },
  { label: 'Founded', value: 'March 2026' },
];

const VARIANTS = [
  {
    heading: 'CrowMark for Suppliers',
    // s.52 and s.71 are duties on the AUTHORITY, so a supplier feature cannot
    // be described as publishing or assessing under either. What a supplier
    // does is produce the evidence, which is what this now says.
    body: 'Tender discovery from Contracts Finder and Find a Tender, the 10% minimum social-value weighting PPN 002 sets on in-scope central government contracts, PPN 017 AI disclosure, and, after award, evidence for the KPIs an authority sets under s.52 and assesses under s.71.',
    href: '/crowmark',
    cta: 'See the supplier side',
  },
  {
    heading: 'CrowMark for Buyers',
    body: 'Publish your requirements against a deterministic social value rubric, then locate the evidence for each one across the responses you receive, quoted verbatim or dropped. CrowMark organises; your evaluation panel scores.',
    href: '/crowmark-buyers',
    cta: 'See the buyer side',
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/about" schema={schema}>
  <Section
    eyebrow="Statute cited · Figures checked · UK built"
    title="Built by bid practitioners who read the rules."
    standfirst="CrowMark starts from the rule that applies and works it the same way every time: find the work, draft answers cited to source, evidence delivery after award. UK public procurement is the worked example because its duties are published."
    id="about-hero"
  
    as="h1">
    <div class="pair">
      {
        APPROACH.map((a) => (
          <div class="pair__card surface surface--pad surface--read">
            <p class="kicker">{a.eyebrow}</p>
            <p class="pair__body">{a.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Mission, vision, values" title="What we stand for." id="principles">
    <ul class="principles">
      {
        PRINCIPLES.map((p) => (
          <li class="principles__item">
            <p class="kicker">{p.kicker}</p>
            <h3 class="principles__h">{p.heading}</h3>
            <p class="principles__body">{p.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="History" title="Where we are." id="timeline">
    <ol class="tl">
      {
        TIMELINE.map((t) => (
          <li class:list={['tl__item', `tl__item--${t.status}`]}>
            <p class="tl__when">{t.when}</p>
            <div class="tl__body">
              <h3 class="tl__h">
                {t.heading}
                {/* Text, not opacity. A planned item must not read as delivered. */}
                <span class:list={['tl__status', `tl__status--${t.status}`]}>
                  {STATUS_LABEL[t.status]}
                </span>
              </h3>
              <p class="tl__p">{t.body}</p>
              {t.link && (
                <p class="tl__p">
                  <a href={t.link.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    {t.link.label}
                  </a>
                </p>
              )}
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Company details" title="CrowAgent Ltd" id="company">
    <dl class="company">
      {
        COMPANY.map((c) => (
          <div class="company__row">
            <dt>{c.label}</dt>
            <dd class={c.mono ? 'is-mono' : undefined}>{c.value}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
      <div class="company__row">
        <dt>Contact</dt>
        <dd><a href="mailto:hello@crowagent.ai">hello@crowagent.ai</a></dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="The stack"
    title="One product, two variants."
    standfirst="CrowMark serves both sides of a procurement, and both work in the public sector and the private sector."
    id="variants"
  >
    <div class="pair">
      {
        VARIANTS.map((v) => (
          <div class="pair__card surface surface--pad surface--read">
            <h3 class="pair__h">{v.heading}</h3>
            <p class="pair__body">{v.body}</p>
            <p class="pair__body">
              <a href={v.href}>{v.cta}</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/*
    CTA COPY, 2026-08-02. The standfirst offered "run the free PPN 002
    Calculator now" while the two buttons below it were a call booking and a
    contact form. The copy promised a third thing that is not on this page.
    Repointed at what the buttons actually do; the buttons are unchanged.
  */}
  <Section
    eyebrow="Ready to start?"
    title="Know the rules better than your competition."
    standfirst="Book a 30-minute call with the team, or send a question and we will answer it. No card, no signup."
    id="about-cta"
  >
    <div class="cta-row">
      <Button href={CALENDLY} target="_blank">Book a 30-minute call</Button>
      <Button href="/contact#contact-form" variant="secondary">Send us a message</Button>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/*
    NEWSLETTER — restored from about.html:422-433, which the first port dropped.
    It is the page's only lead capture that is not a call booking, so losing it
    was a commercial regression rather than a cosmetic one.

    Heading and standfirst are the legacy <h2> and its description verbatim. The
    consent notice is inside NewsletterForm, also verbatim, and points at the
    same /privacy page. The eyebrow is the one addition: this rebuild's head
    block is eyebrow + heading + standfirst, and a head with the capsule missing
    reads as a defect next to every other section on the page.
  */}
  <Section
    eyebrow="Monthly digest"
    title="Get monthly UK procurement digests"
    standfirst="PPN 002 social value, tender-market trends and CrowMark product news, once a month. Unsubscribe any time."
    id="newsletter"
  >
    <NewsletterForm id="about-nl" source="about-newsletter" />
  </Section>
</Base>

<style>
  .kicker {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* Centred to match the head block above it (owner decision, 2026-08-02).
     A flex row defaults to flex-start, so a centred heading sat over a
     left-aligned pair of buttons. This is alignment, not layout: nothing moves
     in the document, the row just centres on the same axis as its heading. */
  .cta-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.75rem; justify-content: center; }

  /* ── Two-up cards, used by both the approach and the variants ─────────── */
  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(20px, 3vw, 36px);
  }

  /* `surface surface--pad` in the markup, not a fourth copy of the recipe. */

  .pair__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .pair__body {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .pair__body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .pair__body a {
    color: var(--c-teal);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.2em;
  }

  .pair__body a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--c-teal); outline-offset: 3px; }

  /* ── Principles ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .principles {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: clamp(20px, 3vw, 40px);
  }

  /* LEFT, DELIBERATELY, and this is the exception the centring rule keeps room
     for. These tiles and the .pair__card blocks above them each carry a five to
     seven line paragraph. Centring a card is right when the card is scanned; at
     five ragged lines it stops being scanned and starts being read, and every
     line then begins in a different place. `surface--read` says the same thing
     for the pair cards; this one is a bordered band rather than a `surface`, so
     it simply keeps the default. */
  .principles__item {
    padding-top: var(--stack-2);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--c-teal);
  }

  .principles__h {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .principles__body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* ── Timeline ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .tl { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }

  .tl__item {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 190px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0 clamp(20px, 3vw, 44px);
    padding: var(--stack-2) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--c-border);
  }

  .tl__when {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .tl__h {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  .tl__p {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .tl__p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .tl__p a { color: var(--c-teal); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 0.2em; }
  .tl__p a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--c-teal); outline-offset: 3px; }

  /* The status is a readable chip, so the distinction survives being unable to
     see the colour it is drawn in. */
  .tl__status {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    padding: 3px 9px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    border: 1px solid var(--c-border);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .tl__status--now {
    border-color: var(--c-border-glow);
    color: var(--c-teal);
  }

  .tl__status--planned {
    border-color: var(--c-orchid);
    color: var(--c-orchid);
  }

  /* ── Company details ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .company { margin: 0; max-width: 720px; }

  .company__row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.2fr);
    gap: 0 1rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-border);
  }

  .company__row:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }

  .company dt {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .company dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .company dd.is-mono { font-family: var(--font-mono); }
  .company dd a { color: var(--c-teal); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 0.2em; }
  .company dd a:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--c-teal); outline-offset: 3px; }

  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .principles { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .pair { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .tl__item { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 6px; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 519px) {
    .company__row { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 4px; }
  }
</style>
